# Security rules — ALWAYS APPLY

Assume every public input and authenticated user can be malicious.

## Identity/authorization
- Authentication is not authorization. Verify ownership/permission for every protected resource/action.
- Avoid IDOR by querying/scoping resources with authenticated owner/permission context.
- Never authorize based only on a client-supplied role/userId/email.

## Input/output
- Strictly validate external payloads; bound body/message/file/context sizes.
- Reject unexpected sensitive fields on mutating security/financial/AI/admin DTOs.
- Encode/sanitize untrusted output; AI/user content is untrusted.
- Never leak raw stack traces, SQL, internal hosts, secrets or provider errors to browser.

## Secrets/privacy
- No API keys/tokens/passwords/cookies/OTP/recovery tokens in logs or URLs.
- Configure structured logger redaction.
- Minimize prompt/content logging by default.

## Abuse/DoS
- Expensive endpoints need rate, concurrency, size and cost caps.
- Fail closed for expensive/security-sensitive actions when critical coordination/security checks are unavailable.
- Add emergency kill switches/circuit breakers for costly external capabilities.

## Web security
- Cookie-authenticated mutations require trusted-origin/CSRF-safe design.
- Use secure headers/CSP/HSTS/etc in production deployment.
- Do not combine wildcard CORS origin with credentials.

## Data/provider security
- Parameterize raw SQL.
- Validate file ownership/type/size before serving/processing uploads.
- URL-fetching/tools must defend against SSRF/private network access when introduced.
- External provider credentials have least privilege and separate environments.

Security-sensitive fixes require regression tests.
